s="anchor doi anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aej.2025.01.021" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="Persistent link using digital object identifier" title="Persistent link using digital object identifier"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aej.2025.01.021</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a><a class="anchor rights-and-content anchor-primary" href="https://s100.copyright.com/AppDispatchServlet?publisherName=ELS&amp;contentID=S1110016825000304&amp;orderBeanReset=true" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Get rights and content</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></div><div class="LicenseInfo text-xs u-margin-xs-bottom"><div class="License"><span>Under a Creative Commons </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">license</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></div><div class="AccessLabel"><span class="access-indicator"></span>Open access</div></div><section class="ReferencedArticles"></section><section class="ReferencedArticles"></section><div class="PageDivider"></div><div class="Abstracts u-font-serif" id="abstracts"><div class="abstract author" id="d1e753"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Abstract</h2><div id="d1e756"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e757"><span><span><span>Real-time safety monitoring on construction sites is essential for ensuring worker safety, but traditional detection methods <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/face" class="topic-link">face</a> challenges in dynamic environments with moving objects, occlusions, and complex conditions. To address these limitations, we propose GS-LinYOLOv10, an improved model based on YOLOv10, specifically designed for drone-based safety monitoring. The GSConv module introduces a lightweight feature extraction mechanism, reducing </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/computational-complexity" class="topic-link">computational complexity</a> without compromising detection accuracy. The Linformer-based </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/attention-machine-learning" class="topic-link">attention mechanism</a> efficiently captures global context, addressing challenges in dynamic and complex environments. The model integrates </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/internet-of-things" class="topic-link">IoT</a><span><span> sensor data for real-time feedback, incorporates the GSConv module for lightweight feature extraction, and utilizes a Linformer-based attention mechanism to efficiently capture global context. These innovations reduce computational complexity while significantly improving detection accuracy. Experimental results show that GS-LinYOLOv10 achieves a precision of 91.2% and a <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/mean-average-precision" class="topic-link">mean average precision</a> (mAP) of 89.4%, outperforming existing models. The integration of </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/internet-of-things" class="topic-link">IoT</a> sensors allows the drone system to dynamically adjust its monitoring focus, improving adaptability to changing environments and enhancing hazard detection. This research provides an advanced, drone-based IoT-enhanced solution for real-time construction site safety monitoring, offering a more effective and efficient approach to safety management.</span></div></div></div></div><ul id="issue-navigation" class="issue-navigation u-margin-s-bottom u-bg-grey1"><li class="previous move-left u-padding-s-ver u-padding-s-left"><a class="button-alternative button-alternative-tertiary u-display-flex button-alternative-icon-left" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S111001682500119X"><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 54 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-left"><path d="M1 61l45-45 7 7-38 38 38 38-7 7z"></path></svg><span class="button-alternative-text-container"><span class="button-alternative-text">Previous <span class="extra-detail-1">article</span><span class="extra-detail-2"> in issue</span></span></span></a></li><li class="next move-right u-padding-s-ver u-padding-s-right"><a class="button-alternative button-alternative-tertiary u-display-flex button-alternative-icon-right" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825001449"><span class="button-alternative-text-container"><span class="button-alternative-text">Next <span class="extra-detail-1">article</span><span class="extra-detail-2"> in issue</span></span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 54 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-right"><path d="M1 99l38-38L1 23l7-7 45 45-45 45z"></path></svg></a></li></ul><div class="Keywords u-font-serif"><div class="keywords-section" id="d1e759" lang="en"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Keywords</h2><div class="keyword" id="d1e762"><span>Drone-based monitoring</span></div><div class="keyword" id="d1e765"><span>Construction site safety</span></div><div class="keyword" id="d1e768"><span>IoT integration</span></div><div class="keyword" id="d1e771"><span>GSConv</span></div><div class="keyword" id="d1e774"><span>Real-time safety detection</span></div><div class="keyword" id="d1e777"><span>Linformer</span></div></div></div><div class="Body u-font-serif" id="body"><div><section id="sec1"><h2 id="d1e798" class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">1. Introduction</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e800">With the rapid advancement of global urbanization, the scale and complexity of construction sites are continuously increasing, leading to growing safety concerns <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b1" name="bb1" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b1"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[1]</span></span></a>. Accidents involving workers, equipment malfunctions, improper operations, and potential safety hazards pose serious risks to construction sites <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b2" name="bb2" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b2"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[2]</span></span></a>. Every year, global construction site accidents cause billions of dollars in economic losses, along with injuries and fatalities. These accidents not only affect project timelines but also threaten workersâ€™ lives and property <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b3" name="bb3" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b3"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[3]</span></span></a>. Therefore, improving construction site safety management and reducing accidents have become critical challenges that urgently need to be addressed. Traditional construction site safety monitoring primarily relies on manual inspections, fixed cameras, and regional sensors. While these methods provide a basic level of safety assurance, they have significant limitations in dynamic and complex environments <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b4" name="bb4" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b4"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[4]</span></span></a>. Manual inspections are time-consuming, can create blind spots, and cannot ensure 24/7 coverage. Fixed monitoring equipment is limited in position, making it challenging to monitor large and complex construction environments effectively, especially with frequent movement of workers and equipment <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b5" name="bb5" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b5"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[5]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b6" name="bb6" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b6"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[6]</span></span></a>. Thus, introducing smarter and more automated technologies to expand coverage and improve real-time monitoring has become a key challenge in construction safety management.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e823"><span><span>In recent years, the rapid development of the <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/internet-of-things" class="topic-link">Internet of Things</a> (IoT) has provided new solutions for construction site safety monitoring. IoT technologies have evolved significantly, integrating advanced sensing, </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/earth-and-planetary-sciences/multisensor-fusion" class="topic-link">data fusion</a>, and communication capabilities </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b7" name="bb7" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b7"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[7]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b8" name="bb8" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b8"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[8]</span></span></a>. For instance, IoT-enabled devices, such as sensors and cameras, are capable of collecting real-time data on environmental conditions, worker activities, and equipment status, transmitting this information to centralized control systems <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b9" name="bb9" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b9"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[9]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b10" name="bb10" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b10"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[10]</span></span></a><span>. These advancements significantly enhance monitoring efficiency and enable timely responses. However, most <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/internet-of-things-device" class="topic-link">IoT devices</a> are installed in fixed locations, limiting their ability to cover entire construction sites comprehensively. In a dynamic and frequently changing environment, fixed monitoring methods fail to track moving workers and equipment effectively, leaving gaps in monitoring dynamic targets </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b11" name="bb11" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b11"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[11]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b12" name="bb12" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b12"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[12]</span></span></a><span>. To address these shortcomings, drone technology has been introduced into construction site safety <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/monitoring-system" class="topic-link">monitoring systems</a> in recent years. Drones have flexible flight capabilities, allowing them to quickly cover different areas of a site and adjust monitoring angles and altitudes as needed, providing comprehensive and dynamic monitoring. Moreover, recent advancements in combining IoT data with drone technology, such as integrating multi-modal sensor data from drones and IoT systems, have further improved the coverage and accuracy of construction site monitoring </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b13" name="bb13" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b13"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[13]</span></span></a>. Equipped with cameras and sensors, drones enable site managers to capture real-time site conditions, particularly in areas that fixed devices cannot cover <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b14" name="bb14" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b14"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[14]</span></span></a><span><span>. However, the vast amount of video and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/image-data" class="topic-link">image data</a> collected by drones during flight presents a significant technical challengeâ€”efficient processing and timely identification of potential hazards remain core issues in drone </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/monitoring-system" class="topic-link">monitoring systems</a> </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b15" name="bb15" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b15"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[15]</span></span></a>. The integration of efficient models, such as Transformer-based architectures, has been explored to handle large-scale video data and improve the detection accuracy of drone systems in real-time scenarios <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b16" name="bb16" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b16"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[16]</span></span></a>.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e854"><span>Object detection technology plays a crucial role in addressing the challenges of <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/image-processing" class="topic-link">processing image</a> and video data. YOLO (You Only Look Once) series models, as mainstream methods in real-time object detection, have been widely applied to complex scenarios due to their outstanding speed and accuracy </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b17" name="bb17" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b17"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[17]</span></span></a>. YOLOv10, as the latest version of the YOLO series, has further optimized both accuracy and inference speed, making it suitable for various real-time monitoring tasks <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b18" name="bb18" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b18"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[18]</span></span></a><span><span>. However, the complex and ever-changing environment of construction sites often involves irregularly shaped objects, complex backgrounds, and occlusions, which places higher demands on YOLOv10 in terms of feature extraction and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/computational-resource" class="topic-link">computational resources</a>. Therefore, increasing computational efficiency while ensuring detection accuracy remains a key challenge for current research. In recent years, researchers have optimized YOLO models from multiple perspectives. To address small object detection in complex scenarios, some studies have introduced multi-scale </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/feature-fusion" class="topic-link">feature fusion</a> modules based on feature pyramid networks (FPN) </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b19" name="bb19" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b19"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[19]</span></span></a><span><span><span>. This approach enhances the modelâ€™s multi-scale feature extraction capability, significantly improving detection of distant and occluded objects. However, although this multi-scale approach improves detection accuracy, it often comes at the cost of increased <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/computational-complexity" class="topic-link">computational complexity</a>, which negatively impacts the modelâ€™s real-time performance. Additionally, for devices with limited resources, improvements like the adoption of </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/depthwise-separable-convolution" class="topic-link">depthwise separable convolutions</a> have successfully reduced the </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/computational-load" class="topic-link">computational load</a> of YOLO models, making them more suitable for deployment on mobile platforms and drones with limited processing power </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b20" name="bb20" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b20"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[20]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b21" name="bb21" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b21"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[21]</span></span></a><span>. However, these lightweight solutions, while enhancing inference speed, may compromise the precision of feature extraction. On the other hand, with the application of Transformer architectures in <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/computervision" class="topic-link">computer vision</a>, the self-attention mechanism has demonstrated its advantages in capturing global feature information </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b22" name="bb22" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b22"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[22]</span></span></a>. Research combining Transformer with YOLO models shows that enhancing the ability to extract global features can effectively improve detection accuracy in complex scenarios <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b23" name="bb23" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b23"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[23]</span></span></a><span>. However, due to the high computational complexity of the Transformerâ€™s self-attention mechanism, these enhanced models still <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/face" class="topic-link">face</a> bottlenecks in inference speed, especially in high-demand, real-time scenarios such as the dynamic environment of construction sites.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e881">Although these approaches have achieved significant progress in terms of accuracy and lightweight design, how to effectively reduce computational complexity while maintaining detection accuracy in complex, dynamic environments like construction sites remains a pressing challenge. Therefore, <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/optimizing-model" class="topic-link">optimizing model</a> architecture to achieve greater detection efficiency and real-time performance has become a core focus of current research in this field.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e883">To improve the application of YOLOv10 in the complex environment of construction sites, this paper proposes an enhanced model named GS-LinYOLOv10. This model incorporates two key <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/optimization-strategy" class="topic-link">optimization strategies</a> to significantly improve computational efficiency while maintaining detection accuracy. First, we introduce the lightweight GSConv module to replace the SCDown module in YOLOv10, thereby reducing computational complexity and improving feature extraction efficiency. Second, we use the Linformer module to replace the traditional Multi-Head Self-Attention (MSHA) mechanism, retaining the feature capturing ability of the self-attention mechanism while greatly reducing computational overhead.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e885">The main contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e887"><ul class="list"><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">â€¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e893">This paper designs and introduces a lightweight GSConv module: this module replaces the SCDown module in YOLOv10, reduces the computational complexity of the model, and significantly improves the efficiency of feature extraction.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">â€¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e898">This paper uses the Linformer module to replace the traditional MSHA mechanism: through this improvement, the computational complexity of the self-attention mechanism is effectively reduced while maintaining the modelâ€™s efficient feature capture capability.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">â€¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e903">This paper experimentally verifies the superior performance of GS-LinYOLOv10 in construction site safety monitoring: experimental results show that the improved model exhibits higher detection accuracy and faster calculation speed in complex dynamic environments.</div></span></li></ul></div></section><section id="sec2"><h2 id="d1e908" class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">2. Related work</h2><section id="sec2.1"><h3 id="d1e913" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">2.1. Advances in object detection models for dynamic environments</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e915">Performing object detection in dynamic environments is a challenging task, as the position, shape, and background of objects in such scenarios are constantly changing, and objects may be subject to occlusions and irregular movements. In recent years, significant progress has been made in object detection models for these complex scenarios, particularly due to the rapid development of <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/deep-learning-model" class="topic-link">deep learning models</a> that have made real-time object detection more feasible.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e917"><span>Initially, <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/convolutional-neural-network" class="topic-link">convolutional neural networks</a> (CNNs) have shown outstanding performance in dynamic environments. CNNs capture both local and global features of objects by extracting multi-level features, making them highly effective for object detection </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b24" name="bb24" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b24"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[24]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b25" name="bb25" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b25"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[25]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b26" name="bb26" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b26"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[26]</span></span></a>. However, in dynamic scenes, objects may be difficult to detect in traditional single-frame image detection due to rapid movement or occlusions. To solve these problems, researchers have introduced improvements, such as incorporating temporal sequence information to enhance model robustness <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b27" name="bb27" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b27"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[27]</span></span></a><span>. By extracting features from consecutive frames and incorporating temporal information, models are better able to handle challenges like <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/motion-blur" class="topic-link">motion blur</a><span> and information loss caused by rapid object movements. In addition, multi-scale feature extraction and fusion are critical for applications in dynamic environments. The introduction of feature pyramid networks (FPN) and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/attention-machine-learning" class="topic-link">attention mechanisms</a> makes models more effective in handling objects of varying scales, especially for detecting distant or occluded objects </span></span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b28" name="bb28" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b28"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[28]</span></span></a><span>. Additionally, some researchers have proposed improved <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/feature-fusion" class="topic-link">feature fusion</a> strategies, such as adding more lateral connections in the feature pyramid to improve information flow across different scales </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b29" name="bb29" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b29"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[29]</span></span></a>. These advancements in feature fusion have greatly enhanced detection accuracy, even in complex environments where objects vary significantly in size and shape.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e936"><span>To meet real-time requirements, many studies have focused on lightweight model optimization and efficiency improvements. Methods such as <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/depthwise-separable-convolution" class="topic-link">depthwise separable convolutions</a><span>, pruning techniques, and quantization are widely used to reduce network <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/computational-complexity" class="topic-link">computational complexity</a>, enabling real-time object detection on resource-constrained devices </span></span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b30" name="bb30" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b30"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[30]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b31" name="bb31" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b31"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[31]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b32" name="bb32" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b32"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[32]</span></span></a>. Lightweight networks such as YOLO and MobileNet have shown significant potential in dynamic environments, reducing computational costs while meeting real-time requirements, particularly in scenarios with strict resource limitations like drones <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b33" name="bb33" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b33"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[33]</span></span></a><span><span>. At the same time, temporal <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/information-processing" class="topic-link">information processing</a> networks like </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/chemical-engineering/recurrent-neural-network" class="topic-link">recurrent neural networks</a><span> (RNNs), long short-term memory (LSTM), and 3D <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/convolutional-network" class="topic-link">convolutional networks</a> have also been applied to object detection in dynamic environments </span></span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b34" name="bb34" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b34"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[34]</span></span></a><span>. These networks model temporal features from video sequences, enhancing the modelâ€™s ability to perceive changes in object <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/behavior-neuroscience" class="topic-link">behavior</a> in dynamic scenes </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b35" name="bb35" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b35"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[35]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b36" name="bb36" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b36"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[36]</span></span></a><span>. Such approaches effectively reduce <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/error-detection" class="topic-link">detection errors</a> caused by rapid object movement or occlusion, which are common in dynamic environments.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e955"><span>Multi-modal <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/earth-and-planetary-sciences/multisensor-fusion" class="topic-link">data fusion</a><span> is also a key approach for object detection in dynamic environments. By integrating data from different sensors, such as <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/rgb-image" class="topic-link">RGB images</a>, depth maps, LiDAR, and infrared images, object detection models can gain a more comprehensive understanding of complex scenes, improving detection accuracy and robustness for moving targets </span></span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b37" name="bb37" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b37"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[37]</span></span></a>. For instance, combining infrared data with visible light can significantly enhance detection capabilities in low-light or highly occluded conditions <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b38" name="bb38" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b38"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[38]</span></span></a><span>. Moreover, researchers have investigated using online learning and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/biochemistry-genetics-and-molecular-biology/transfer-of-learning" class="topic-link">transfer learning</a> techniques to make object detection models more adaptable in changing environments </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b39" name="bb39" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b39"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[39]</span></span></a>. Online learning allows models to update themselves continuously during detection, making them more responsive to new targets and conditions <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b40" name="bb40" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b40"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[40]</span></span></a>. Transfer learning, on the other hand, uses pre-trained models to transfer knowledge from related tasks, reducing training time and improving detection outcomes <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b41" name="bb41" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b41"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[41]</span></span></a>. These methods highlight the importance of adaptability in ensuring consistent detection performance across diverse and evolving scenarios.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e978">In summary, advancements in object detection for dynamic environments have addressed many key challenges, but issues such as computational inefficiency, difficulty in capturing global contextual information, and adaptability in resource-constrained scenarios remain significant. Further exploration is required to develop lightweight architectures and efficient attention mechanisms that integrate multi-modal data for better performance in real-time applications.</div></section><section id="sec2.2"><h3 id="d1e983" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">2.2. Optimization and application of YOLO series in real-time monitoring</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e985"><span>The YOLO (You Only Look Once) models are highly favored in monitoring and security tasks due to their ability to perform real-time object detection. One of the <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/defining-feature" class="topic-link">defining features</a> of YOLO is its single-stage detection method, which allows both object classification and localization to be carried out in a single step, making it exceptionally fast </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b42" name="bb42" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b42"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[42]</span></span></a>. This speed, along with relatively low computational requirements, makes YOLO particularly suitable for environments that require quick responses, like construction sites, where efficiency is crucial. These advantages make YOLO models a go-to choice for scenarios involving frequent changes and high real-time requirements.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e991"><span><span>In recent years, the YOLO series has undergone multiple updates and optimizations, further improving its performance in complex environments. Versions from YOLOv4 to YOLOv7 have all achieved significant progress in both detection accuracy and inference speed. Specifically, YOLOv7 introduces an improved <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/feature-extraction-network" class="topic-link">feature extraction network</a>, Cross-Stage Partial (CSP) connections, and advanced </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/activation-function" class="topic-link">activation functions</a>, all of which enhance the modelâ€™s feature extraction capabilities and detection precision for small objects </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b43" name="bb43" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b43"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[43]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b44" name="bb44" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b44"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[44]</span></span></a>. These improvements allow YOLOv7 to maintain efficient and stable detection performance in multi-object and dynamically changing environments such as construction sites. For instance, recent studies have demonstrated YOLOv7â€™s ability to accurately detect workers and machinery, even in environments with frequent occlusions and irregular object movements, proving its robustness in real-world scenarios. A concrete example of such an application is a large-scale construction site safety monitoring project, which utilizes YOLOv7 to detect the positions of personnel and equipment at the site <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b45" name="bb45" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b45"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[45]</span></span></a>. The construction site is divided into multiple areas, each with varying progress, and frequent movement of equipment and personnel makes traditional fixed camera surveillance insufficient for complete coverage.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1001"><span>In addition, to enhance the applicability of YOLO models in resource-constrained environments, multiple lightweight technologies have been introduced. For instance, methods such as pruning, quantization, and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/knowledge-distillation" class="topic-link">knowledge distillation</a> have been applied to significantly reduce the parameter and computation requirements of YOLO models, making them suitable for deployment on embedded devices and drones </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b46" name="bb46" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b46"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[46]</span></span></a>, <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b47" name="bb47" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b47"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[47]</span></span></a><span><span>. In certain <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/biochemistry-genetics-and-molecular-biology/remote-sensing" class="topic-link">remote monitoring</a> projects, lightweight versions like YOLOv5-Tiny have been used for real-time object detection. For example, in mountainous highway construction projects, low-power cameras and </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/edge-computing" class="topic-link">edge computing</a> devices have been deployed to use YOLOv5-Tiny for real-time detection of vehicles and personnel in the construction area, ensuring the safety and order of the site </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b48" name="bb48" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b48"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[48]</span></span></a><span>. These lightweight YOLO variants perform well in applications requiring mobile monitoring, such as construction sites, effectively enhancing real-time monitoring capabilities while reducing <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/computational-resource" class="topic-link">computational resource</a> demands.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1013"><span>In practical applications, YOLO models are often combined with edge computing platforms to further improve the efficiency of data processing. By performing object detection and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/materials-science/initial-datum" class="topic-link">initial data</a><span> processing locally, dependence on remote servers can be reduced, thus shortening <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/response-time" class="topic-link">response times</a> </span></span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b49" name="bb49" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b49"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[49]</span></span></a>. This is especially important in scenarios like construction site safety monitoring, which require high levels of real-time responsiveness. For instance, in a smart construction site project, edge computing nodes are combined with YOLOv7 for real-time monitoring, with the edge nodes directly analyzing the data from cameras and issuing alerts when workers are detected without wearing proper safety equipment, thereby preventing safety incidents <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b50" name="bb50" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b50"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[50]</span></span></a><span>. Additionally, some studies have integrated attention mechanisms with YOLO models to improve detection accuracy in complex backgrounds, particularly in cases involving multiple object occlusions and poor <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/lighting-condition" class="topic-link">lighting conditions</a>, effectively enhancing the reliability of YOLO </span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#b51" name="bb51" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="b51"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">[51]</span></span></a>.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1027">In summary, while the YOLO series has achieved remarkable success in real-time monitoring applications through improvements in model structure, lightweight strategies, and edge computing integration, challenges such as handling occlusions, improving detection accuracy in dynamic environments, and balancing computational efficiency remain significant. Lightweight variants often compromise precision in complex scenarios, making them less suitable for demanding applications like construction site monitoring. To address these limitations, this paper introduces GS-LinYOLOv10, which incorporates GSConv for efficient feature extraction, Linformer for capturing global context, and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/internet-of-things" class="topic-link">IoT</a> integration for dynamic adaptability. The next section provides a detailed explanation of the proposed model.</div></section></section><section id="sec3"><h2 id="d1e1032" class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">3. Method</h2><section id="sec3.1"><h3 id="d1e1037" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">3.1. Overview of GS-LinYOLOv10 architecture</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1039">GS-LinYOLOv10 is an enhanced version of the YOLOv10 model, tailored specifically for real-time monitoring in challenging environments like construction sites. In this context, drones equipped with cameras, along with IoT (Internet of Things) devices, play a vital role in improving <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/safety-measure" class="topic-link">safety measures</a><span>. GS-LinYOLOv10 retains the rapid detection abilities of YOLOv10 while being optimized to work seamlessly with IoT, resulting in better detection speed and accuracy. These improvements are achieved through <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/structural-adjustment" class="topic-link">structural adjustments</a> and replacing specific modules, yet the model maintains the end-to-end detection approach typical of YOLO models.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1041"><span><span>The architecture of GS-LinYOLOv10 consists of three main components: the Backbone, the Neck, and the Head. The Backbone is responsible for feature extraction from the images captured by the drones, using <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/convolutional-layer" class="topic-link">convolutional layers</a> combined with GSConv modules. GSConv acts as a lightweight alternative to traditional </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/convolution-operation" class="topic-link">convolution operations</a><span>, replacing the SCDown module to decrease <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/computational-load" class="topic-link">computational load</a> without compromising feature extraction quality. This is particularly important in construction sites where drones have limited computational resources but must still handle real-time data processing in a dynamic environment. Furthermore, IoT sensors scattered throughout the site continuously provide real-time data, such as worker movements, equipment statuses, and environmental conditions. By integrating this data with drone imagery, the system achieves a richer situational understanding. </span></span><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#fig1" name="bfig1" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="fig1"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Fig. 1</span></span></a> provides an overview of how the GS-LinYOLOv10 system brings together data from multiple sources, including drones and IoT sensors, into a unified framework. The figure illustrates how real-time information from workers, equipment, and sensors is gathered and analyzed by GS-LinYOLOv10, enhancing both safety monitoring and decision-making on-site.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom"><div id="d1e1047"><span><span>GS-LinYOLOv10 uses a custom feature fusion module to effectively fuse features from different stages of Backbone, improving the modelâ€™s ability to detect objects of different sizes, especially in the case of occlusion or small targets, greatly improving detection accuracy. At the same time, IoT sensor data distributed at the construction site (such as motion and environmental sensor data) also participates in feature fusion, combining the droneâ€™s visual data with contextual information such as temperature or equipment status, thereby building a more comprehensive system that can identify potential safety risks that are difficult for cameras to capture. The Head module of GS-LinYOLOv10 generates the final detection output, including <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/bounding-box" class="topic-link">bounding boxes</a>, category labels, and confidence scores. Its innovation lies in the integration of a Linformer-based </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/attention-machine-learning" class="topic-link">attention mechanism</a> in Backbone, which improves the ability to capture long-distance dependencies without increasing computational costs. This is particularly important for drone monitoring tasks, ensuring fast processing capabilities when performing real-time safety hazard detection. In addition, IoT data can dynamically adjust the detection focus. For example, when the sensor detects abnormal vibration or high temperature in a specific area, the drone can adjust the flight path to focus on that area, thereby improving safety monitoring efficiency and speeding up response.</span></div><figure class="figure text-xs" id="fig1"><span><img src="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr1.jpg" height="258" alt="" aria-describedby="d1e21"><ol class="u-margin-s-bottom"><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr1_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download high-res image (313KB)"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download high-res image (313KB)</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr1.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download full-size image"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download full-size image</span></span></span></a></li></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span id="d1e21"><p id="d1e22"><span class="label">Fig. 1</span>. The overall workflow of the GS-LinYOLOv10 system, integrating drone-based video data and IoT sensor inputs for construction site safety monitoring. The model collects, accumulates, and analyzes data in real time from various sources such as workers, construction equipment, and environmental sensors, enhancing detection accuracy and response times for potential hazards.</p></span></span></figure></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1051">As shown in <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#fig2" name="bfig2" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="fig2"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Fig. 2</span></span></a><span>, the complete structure of the GS-LinYOLOv10 model shows the flow of data in the Backbone, Neck, and Head, with a focus on the integration of the GSConv and Linformer modules. GS-LinYOLOv10 provides a solution that strikes a balance between detection accuracy and computational efficiency. With IoT integration, the system is able to receive data from sensors in real time, allowing it to better adapt to dynamic construction environments. These enhancements significantly improve the systemâ€™s performance in real-time scenarios, such as construction site safety monitoring and automated monitoring, effectively detecting potential safety hazards â€” including mobile machinery and workers in dangerous areas â€” while maintaining low latency and efficient <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/resource-utilisation" class="topic-link">resource utilization</a>.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom"><div id="d1e1057"></div><figure class="figure text-xs" id="fig2"><span><img src="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr2.jpg" height="478" alt="" aria-describedby="d1e29"><ol class="u-margin-s-bottom"><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr2_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download high-res image (796KB)"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download high-res image (796KB)</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr2.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download full-size image"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download full-size image</span></span></span></a></li></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span id="d1e29"><p id="d1e30"><span class="label">Fig. 2</span>. The architecture of the GS-LinYOLOv10 model, illustrating the flow of data through the Backbone, Neck, and Head components. The Backbone consists of convolutional layers integrated with GSConv modules to efficiently extract features from input images (drone data and IoT sensor data). The Neck is responsible for feature fusion across different scales, while the Head generates the final predictions, including bounding boxes, class labels, and confidence scores. The integration of Linformer and PSA modules in the attention mechanism enhances the modelâ€™s ability to handle long-range dependencies, improving both detection accuracy and computational efficiency.</p></span></span></figure></div></section><section id="sec3.2"><h3 id="d1e1063" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">3.2. Gsconv integration for lightweight feature extraction</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1065">In the GS-LinYOLOv10 model, feature extraction is crucial for ensuring both the accuracy and speed of object detection. To optimize this process, GS-LinYOLOv10 replaces the SCDown module from the original YOLOv10 with the GSConv module. GSConv, short for Ghost <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/spatial-convolution" class="topic-link">Spatial Convolution</a><span><span>, is specifically designed to enhance efficiency by eliminating redundant computations often found in traditional convolution operations, thereby reducing computational overhead without sacrificing detection quality. This makes it highly suitable for lightweight and efficient processing, which is vital for resource-constrained applications such as drone-based monitoring. The core idea of GSConv is to eliminate redundant computations that are often present in traditional convolution operations. Standard <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/convolution-layer" class="topic-link">convolution layers</a> tend to generate many repetitive feature maps, adding unnecessary computational overhead without significantly enhancing the output. GSConv addresses this issue by first creating a smaller set of core feature maps, and then using simple </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/linear-transformation" class="topic-link">linear transformations</a> to expand those features. This design ensures that key feature information is preserved while minimizing the computational cost, maintaining both high accuracy and inference speed. The process can be described mathematically as follows:</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1067">First, the input <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-1-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.98ex" height="1.971ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 852.5 848.5" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-58"></use></g></g></svg></span></span> is passed through a standard convolution operation to generate the initial set of feature maps <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-2-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span>: <span class="display"><span id="fd1" class="formula"><span class="label">(1)</span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-3-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="12.926ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 5565.3 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(1313,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-3D"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(2369,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-58"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(3444,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-2217"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(4166,0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-57"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(944,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span></span></span>where <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-4-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span> is the set of initial feature maps generated from the input <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-5-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.98ex" height="1.971ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 852.5 848.5" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-58"></use></g></g></svg></span></span><span> using <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/convolution-kernel" class="topic-link">convolution kernel</a> </span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-6-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="3.248ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1398.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-57"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(944,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span>, and <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-7-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.162ex" height="1.394ex" viewBox="0 -548.5 500.5 600.2" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: 0.079ex; margin-bottom: -0.199ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-2217"></use></g></g></svg></span></span> represents the convolution operation.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1145">Next, the initial feature maps <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-8-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span> are transformed by applying a convolution with a second kernel <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-9-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="3.248ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1398.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-57"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(944,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span>, followed by a non-linear <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/activation-function" class="topic-link">activation function</a> </span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style="display: none;"></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-10-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.33ex" height="1.394ex" viewBox="0 -498.8 572.5 600.2" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-3C3"></use></g></g></svg></span></span>. The result is added back to <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-11-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span>, forming the expanded feature maps <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-12-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span>: <span class="display"><span id="fd2" class="formula"><span class="label">(2)</span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-13-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="22.122ex" height="2.779ex" viewBox="0 -846.5 9524.7 1196.3" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.812ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(1313,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-3D"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(2369,0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(3627,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-2B"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(4627,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-3C3"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(5366,0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-28"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(389,0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(1647,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-2217"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(2369,0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-57"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(944,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(3768,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-29"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span></span></span>where <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-14-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span> is the expanded feature map set, <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-15-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="3.248ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1398.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-57"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(944,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span> is a linear transformation applied to <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-16-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span>, and <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-17-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.33ex" height="1.394ex" viewBox="0 -498.8 572.5 600.2" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-3C3"></use></g></g></svg></span></span><span> represents a non-linear <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/activation-function" class="topic-link">activation function</a>, such as ReLU.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1280">Finally, the original feature maps <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-18-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span> are concatenated with the expanded feature maps <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-19-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span><span>, producing the final set of <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/output-feature-map" class="topic-link">output feature maps</a> </span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-20-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.773ex" height="1.855ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 763.5 798.9" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.12ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g></svg></span></span>: <span class="display"><span id="fd3" class="formula"><span class="label">(3)</span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-21-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="12.009ex" height="2.779ex" viewBox="0 -846.5 5170.5 1196.3" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.812ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(1041,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-3D"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(2097,0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-5B"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(278,0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(1313,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-2C"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(1759,0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(2794,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-5D"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span></span></span>where <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-22-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.773ex" height="1.855ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 763.5 798.9" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.12ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g></svg></span></span> is the final set of output feature maps, formed by concatenating the original feature maps <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-23-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-31"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span> with the expanded feature maps <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-24-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.405ex" height="2.317ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 1035.4 997.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-59"></use></g></g><g is="true" transform="translate(581,-150)"><g is="true"><use transform="scale(0.707)" xlink:href="#MJMAIN-32"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></span></span>.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1368"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#fig3" name="bfig3" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="fig3"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Fig. 3</span></span></a><span> illustrates the structure of the GSConv module. As shown in the diagram, GSConv processes the input image through a lightweight convolution layer to generate initial feature maps, which are then expanded using <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/linear-transformation" class="topic-link">linear transformations</a>. This streamlined approach ensures computational efficiency without compromising the modelâ€™s ability to capture detailed features. This design allows GSConv to capture key features while minimizing the computational burden, enabling efficient feature extraction without compromising on performance.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom"><div id="d1e1373">The introduction of the GSConv module not only reduces computational cost but also improves the inference speed of the model. This is particularly critical in dynamic environments like construction sites, where drones require rapid data processing to provide real-time safety monitoring. The reduced latency achieved by GSConv ensures faster responses to environmental changes, enabling more effective hazard detection. Even when handling high-resolution inputs, the GSConv module maintains a high level of efficiency, making the model well-suited for handling the complex and dynamic nature of construction site environments, where objects such as workers and machinery need to be detected swiftly. Additionally, the GSConv module excels at retaining detailed features, which is critical in detecting small or partially occluded objects that are often present in construction sites. The unique feature generation mechanism of GSConv ensures precise detection of these challenging targets, enhancing the robustness of the model in complex environments.</div><figure class="figure text-xs" id="fig3"><span><img src="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr3.jpg" height="186" alt="" aria-describedby="d1e37"><ol class="u-margin-s-bottom"><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr3_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download high-res image (205KB)"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download high-res image (205KB)</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr3.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download full-size image"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download full-size image</span></span></span></a></li></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span id="d1e37"><p id="d1e38"><span class="label">Fig. 3</span>. The structure of GSConv, showcasing the convolutional process, depth-wise convolution (DWConv), contact, and shuffle operations. The architecture efficiently processes feature maps by separating the depth-wise and point-wise convolutions to improve computational efficiency and feature representation.</p></span></span></figure></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1377">In summary, the integration of the GSConv module is central to the lightweight feature extraction process of GS-LinYOLOv10. By reducing the number of parameters and computational cost, the GSConv module significantly boosts the modelâ€™s overall performance, making it highly efficient for use in resource-limited environments, such as drone-based <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/monitoring-system" class="topic-link">monitoring systems</a>. This balance between efficiency and accuracy makes GS-LinYOLOv10 an ideal choice for real-time monitoring tasks in construction sites and other complex environments.</div></section><section id="sec3.3"><h3 id="d1e1382" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">3.3. Linformer-based efficient attention mechanism</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1384"><span>In the GS-LinYOLOv10 model, a critical improvement is the introduction of a Linformer-based attention mechanism in place of the traditional Multi-ead <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/earth-and-planetary-sciences/self-attention" class="topic-link">Self Attention</a> (MSHA). This modification significantly reduces computational complexity while maintaining the ability to capture global context in feature maps, making it ideal for real-time applications such as drone-based monitoring. Linformer, a lightweight variant of the Transformer architecture, reduces the complexity of the attention operation from quadratic to linear, making it well-suited for real-time applications such as drone-based monitoring in construction sites. Traditional self-attention mechanisms excel at capturing global context but come at a high computational cost, particularly when dealing with high-resolution images. Specifically, MSHA has a quadratic complexity with respect to the input </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/sequence-length" class="topic-link">sequence length</a>, making it computationally expensive for real-time detection tasks in resource-constrained environments, such as drones. Linformer overcomes this challenge by approximating the full attention matrix with a low-rank factorization, significantly reducing the attention mechanismâ€™s computational cost while maintaining effective feature extraction.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1386">Linformer achieves this by projecting keys and values into lower-dimensional spaces using linear projections, reducing the size of the attention matrix without sacrificing the necessary information. The <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/mathematical-process" class="topic-link">mathematical process</a> underlying Linformer can be described as follows:</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e1388">First, given an input sequence <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style="display: none;"></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-25-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.98ex" height="1.971ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 852.5 848.5" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-58"></use></g></g></svg></span></span>, representing the feature map, the keys <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-26-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="2.066ex" height="1.971ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 889.5 848.5" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-4B"></use></g></g></svg></span></span>, queries <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-27-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.838ex" height="2.432ex" viewBox="0 -796.9 791.5 1047.3" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.582ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-51"></use></g></g></svg></span></span>, and values <span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-28-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.787ex" height="1.971ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 769.5 848.5" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-56"></use></g></g></svg></span></span><span> are derived by applying <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/earth-and-planetary-sciences/linear-transformation" class="topic-link">linear transformations</a> to </span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-29-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="1.98ex" height="1.971ex" viewBox="0 -747.2 852.5 848.5" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.235ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-58"></use></g></g></svg></span></span>: <span class="display"><span id="fd4" class="formula"><span class="label">(4)</span><span class="math"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style=""></span><span class="MathJax_SVG" id="MathJax-Element-30-Frame" tabindex="0" style="font-size: 90%; display: inline-block;"><svg xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" width="38.942ex" height="2.663ex" viewBox="0 -796.9 16766.8 1146.6" role="img" focusable="false" style="vertical-align: -0.812ex;"><g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-51"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(1069,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMAIN-3D"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(2125,0)"><use xlink:href="#MJMATHI-58"></use></g><g is="true" transform="translate(2978,0)"><g is="true"><g is="true"><use xl