.1</td><td class="align-left">87.9</td></tr><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">Improved yolov3</th><td class="align-left">12.11</td><td class="align-left">40.51</td><td class="align-left">13.33</td><td class="align-left">80.11</td><td class="align-left">14.2</td><td class="align-left">79.5</td></tr><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">YOLO-v4</th><td class="align-left">13.53</td><td class="align-left">45.23</td><td class="align-left">14.12</td><td class="align-left">75.83</td><td class="align-left">14.9</td><td class="align-left">74.7</td></tr><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">YOLO-DS</th><td class="align-left">8.44</td><td class="align-left">33.81</td><td class="align-left">11.71</td><td class="align-left">82.62</td><td class="align-left">12.8</td><td class="align-left">81.4</td></tr><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">GS-LinYOLOv10</th><td class="align-left">10.72</td><td class="align-left">38.93</td><td class="align-left">9.81</td><td class="align-left">92.41</td><td class="align-left">10.5</td><td class="align-left">91.8</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom"><div id="d1e2054"></div><figure class="figure text-xs" id="fig5"><span><img src="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr5.jpg" height="310" alt="" aria-describedby="d1e68"><ol class="u-margin-s-bottom"><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr5_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download high-res image (313KB)"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download high-res image (313KB)</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr5.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download full-size image"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download full-size image</span></span></span></a></li></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span id="d1e68"><p id="d1e69"><span class="label">Fig. 5</span>. mAP50 and mAP50-95 curves of YOLOv10 and GS-LinYOLOv10 on Kaggle Construction Site Safety Dataset over 50 epochs.</p></span></span></figure></div></section><section id="sec5.2"><h3 id="d1e2060" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">5.2. Performance comparison of GSO-YOLO on kaggle construction site safety dataset</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2062">The <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#fig5" name="bfig5" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="fig5"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Fig. 5</span></span></a><span> clearly shows that GS-LinYOLOv10 outperforms YOLOv10 on the Construction Site Safety Dataset, particularly in the key metrics of mAP50 and mAP50-95. GS-LinYOLOv10 demonstrates a faster <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/performance-improvement" class="topic-link">performance improvement</a> during the first 10 epochs, indicating quicker convergence compared to YOLOv10, which progresses more slowly. This suggests that GS-LinYOLOv10 reaches higher detection accuracy earlier in the training process, which is crucial for real-time detection tasks in complex environments like construction sites. As training progresses, the performance gap between GS-LinYOLOv10 and YOLOv10 continues to widen. By the 50th epoch, GS-LinYOLOv10 achieves an mAP50 of 89.4%, which is 8.2 percentage points higher than YOLOv10â€™s 81.2%. This reflects its superior accuracy in detecting objects in challenging environments. Furthermore, GS-LinYOLOv10â€™s mAP50-95 reaches 72.1%, significantly higher than YOLOv10â€™s 64.3%, showing stronger generalization capabilities when handling varying IoU thresholds. The advantages of GS-LinYOLOv10 stem from the integration of the GSConv and Linformer modules, which improve its ability to capture richer contextual information while maintaining computational efficiency. This not only accelerates the early convergence but also ensures consistent performance gains throughout training. Overall, GS-LinYOLOv10 proves to be a more efficient and accurate model for real-time monitoring in complex construction site scenarios, outperforming YOLOv10.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom"><div id="d1e2070">As shown in <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#fig6" name="bfig6" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="fig6"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Fig. 6</span></span></a><span>, the mAP50 and mAP50-95 curves for GSO-YOLO and YOLOv10 on the Construction Site Safety Dataset illustrate the performance differences between the two models across various categories. Overall, GSO-YOLO outperforms YOLOv10 in most categories, with particularly strong performance in key categories such as <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/excavators" class="topic-link">Excavator</a>, Hardhat, and Safety Vest, demonstrating its advantages in complex construction site environments. The figure clearly shows that GSO-YOLO consistently achieves higher mAP50 values than YOLOv10 across most categories. This reflects the improvements made by integrating the GSConv and Linformer Attention modules, which enhance GSO-YOLOâ€™s ability to capture detailed and contextual information about targets. This is especially evident when detecting large construction equipment such as dump trucks and trailers, where GSO-YOLO exhibits significantly better performance compared to YOLOv10. However, YOLOv10 shows more comparable performance in certain categories, such as van and Person, indicating that it still maintains strong detection capabilities for more common objects. In summary, GSO-YOLOâ€™s advantage lies in its ability to better handle detection tasks in complex scenarios, particularly in environments like construction sites, where accurate identification of safety equipment and large machinery is critical. These results confirm GSO-YOLOâ€™s notable improvements and its potential as a reliable model for construction site safety monitoring.</span></div><figure class="figure text-xs" id="fig6"><span><img src="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr6.jpg" height="313" alt="" aria-describedby="d1e77"><ol class="u-margin-s-bottom"><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr6_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download high-res image (428KB)"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download high-res image (428KB)</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr6.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download full-size image"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download full-size image</span></span></span></a></li></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span id="d1e77"><p id="d1e78"><span class="label">Fig. 6</span>. Comparison of mAP50 and mAP50-95 across different categories for YOLOv10 and GSO-YOLO on the Construction Site Safety Dataset.</p></span></span></figure></div></section><section id="sec5.3"><h3 id="d1e2081" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">5.3. Ablation study</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2083"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#tbl4" name="btbl4" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="tbl4"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Table 4</span></span></a> presents the results of the ablation study on the COCO Dataset and Kaggle Construction Site Safety Dataset to assess the impact of GSConv and Linformer Attention modules on the GS-LinYOLOv10 model. The full GS-LinYOLOv10 model performs best across all versions, with 91.2% precision and 89.4% mAP50 on the COCO dataset, and 92.1% precision with 90.3% mAP50 on the Kaggle dataset. This demonstrates the combined importance of GSConv and Linformer Attention for accurate detection and classification in diverse environments. Removing the GSConv module leads to significant performance drops, with COCO mAP50 decreasing to 85.2% and Kaggle mAP50 to 86.1%. This indicates that GSConv greatly enhances feature extraction and efficiency. Removing Linformer Attention also reduces performance, with COCO mAP50 dropping to 87.3% and Kaggle mAP50 to 88%. While the impact is smaller than removing GSConv, Linformer Attention clearly improves the modelâ€™s ability to capture long-range dependencies, which is crucial for complex detection tasks. The baseline YOLOv10 model performs reasonably well (mAP50 of 81.2% on COCO and 82.3% on Kaggle), but it is outperformed by the full GS-LinYOLOv10, particularly in handling varied object sizes and complexities. In summary, the study shows that both GSConv and Linformer Attention are crucial to achieving the highest detection accuracy, and their combination in the full GS-LinYOLOv10 model results in superior performance on both datasets.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom"><div id="d1e2088"></div><div class="tables colsep-0 frame-topbot rowsep-0" id="tbl4"><span class="captions text-s"><span id="d1e506"><p id="d1e507"><span class="label">Table 4</span>. Ablation Study Results on COCO and Kaggle Construction Site Safety Datasets.</p></span></span><div class="groups"><table><thead><tr class="valign-top"><th scope="col" class="align-left">Models</th><th scope="col" class="align-left rowsep-1" colspan="5">COCO dataset</th><th scope="col" class="align-left rowsep-1" colspan="5">Kaggle construction site safety dataset</th></tr><tr class="rowsep-1 valign-top"><td scope="col" class="align-left"><span class="screen-reader-only">Empty Cell</span></td><th scope="col" class="align-left">Precision</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">Recall</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">F1-Score</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">mAP50</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">mAP50-95</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">Precision</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">Recall</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">F1-Score</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">mAP50</th><th scope="col" class="align-left">mAP50-95</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">YOLOv10 (Baseline)</th><td class="align-left">83.5</td><td class="align-left">80.7</td><td class="align-left">82.1</td><td class="align-left">81.2</td><td class="align-left">64.3</td><td class="align-left">84.2</td><td class="align-left">81.1</td><td class="align-left">82.6</td><td class="align-left">82.3</td><td class="align-left">66.5</td></tr><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">Without GSConv</th><td class="align-left">87.5</td><td class="align-left">84.3</td><td class="align-left">85.9</td><td class="align-left">85.2</td><td class="align-left">68.7</td><td class="align-left">88.0</td><td class="align-left">85.2</td><td class="align-left">86.5</td><td class="align-left">86.1</td><td class="align-left">70.3</td></tr><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">Without Linformer Attention</th><td class="align-left">89.4</td><td class="align-left">86.8</td><td class="align-left">88.1</td><td class="align-left">87.3</td><td class="align-left">70.5</td><td class="align-left">90.1</td><td class="align-left">87.3</td><td class="align-left">88.7</td><td class="align-left">88.0</td><td class="align-left">72.1</td></tr><tr class="valign-top"><th class="align-left" scope="row">Full GS-LinYOLOv10 Model</th><td class="align-left">91.2</td><td class="align-left">88.9</td><td class="align-left">90.0</td><td class="align-left">89.4</td><td class="align-left">72.1</td><td class="align-left">92.1</td><td class="align-left">89.5</td><td class="align-left">90.8</td><td class="align-left">90.3</td><td class="align-left">74.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></section><section id="sec5.4"><h3 id="d1e2094" class="rx-font-18 u-margin-m-top u-margin-xs-bottom">5.4. Visualization of detection results on construction safety dataset</h3><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2096">In <a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1110016825000304?ref=pdf_download&amp;fr=RR-2&amp;rr=a2439b1b7e3d4206#fig7" name="bfig7" data-sd-ui-side-panel-opener="true" data-xocs-content-type="reference" data-xocs-content-id="fig7"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Fig. 7</span></span></a><span><span>, we present the detection results of the GS-LinYOLOv10 model on a construction safety dataset. The model successfully detects and labels various objects, including workers, equipment, and safety gear such as hard hats, safety vests, and gloves. In the top-left image, the model accurately identifies a worker on a ladder, correctly flagging the <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/epileptic-absence" class="topic-link">absence</a><span> of a hard hat and safety vest, demonstrating its ability to detect safety violations. In the center image, a worker is fully equipped with safety gear, including a hard hat, safety vest, mask, and gloves, all of which are correctly detected and labeled, highlighting the precision of the model. In the top-right image, the model successfully detects two workers <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/handling-equipment" class="topic-link">handling equipment</a> and correctly identifies their safety gear. The bottom three images showcase the modelâ€™s ability to detect construction machinery, such as excavators, wheel loaders, and dump trucks. The model effectively recognizes these large vehicles in complex backgrounds, while also accurately labeling distant workers. These results illustrate that the GS-LinYOLOv10 model performs well in detecting a variety of object sizes and types, particularly in scenarios where both personnel and machinery coexist on construction sites. The modelâ€™s outstanding performance is demonstrated in its ability to detect safety violations and maintain high detection accuracy under </span></span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/challenging-condition" class="topic-link">challenging conditions</a>, including low lighting, complex backgrounds, and varying object sizes. This robustness is particularly important for ensuring construction site safety.</span></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom"><div id="d1e2102"></div><figure class="figure text-xs" id="fig7"><span><img src="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr7.jpg" height="384" alt="" aria-describedby="d1e85"><ol class="u-margin-s-bottom"><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr7_lrg.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download high-res image (1MB)"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download high-res image (1MB)</span></span></span></a></li><li><a class="anchor download-link u-font-sans anchor-primary" href="https://ars.els-cdn.com/content/image/1-s2.0-S1110016825000304-gr7.jpg" target="_blank" download="" title="Download full-size image"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Download: <span class="download-link-title">Download full-size image</span></span></span></a></li></ol></span><span class="captions text-s"><span id="d1e85"><p id="d1e86"><span class="label">Fig. 7</span>. Visualization of detection results by GS-LinYOLOv10 on the construction safety dataset.</p></span></span></figure></div></section></section><section id="sec6"><h2 id="d1e2108" class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">6. Conclusion</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2110">This study proposes GS-LinYOLOv10, an improved solution to the challenges of real-time object detection and safety monitoring in dynamic construction site environments. By integrating IoT sensor data, the model enhances contextual awareness and detection accuracy, addressing limitations of existing solutions in handling frequent movement and occlusion. GS-LinYOLOv10 employs the GSConv module for efficient feature extraction and a Linformer-based attention mechanism to effectively capture global context. Experimental results on the COCO and Kaggle Construction Site Safety datasets demonstrate that GS-LinYOLOv10 outperforms existing YOLO versions in precision, recall, F1-score, and inference speed. Its lightweight design and IoT integration make it highly suitable for resource-constrained, real-time monitoring applications.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2112"><span>Despite these advancements, the model still <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/neuroscience/face" class="topic-link">faces</a> challenges in detecting extremely small objects and distant targets, which remain difficult due to the inherent limitations of feature extraction in such cases. Additionally, prolonged IoT-based operations may lead to increased </span><a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/electric-power-utilization" class="topic-link">power consumption</a>, potentially limiting deployment on low-power devices.</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2114">Future work will focus on addressing these challenges. To enhance small object detection, we will explore multi-scale feature extraction techniques and refine the modelâ€™s capability to handle distant or partially obscured objects. To improve energy efficiency, advanced optimization techniques such as model quantization, pruning, and adaptive learning algorithms will be investigated. Expanding the system to include additional IoT <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/data-source" class="topic-link">data sources</a><span>, such as <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/thermal-sensor" class="topic-link">thermal sensors</a> and LiDAR, will improve detection accuracy in low-visibility conditions. These advancements will broaden the applicability of GS-LinYOLOv10 to more diverse and challenging environments.</span></div></section><section id="d1e2116"><h2 id="d1e2117" class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">CRediT authorship contribution statement</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2119"><strong>Yang Song:</strong> Writing â€“ original draft, Methodology, Conceptualization. <strong>ZhenLin Chen:</strong><span> Supervision, Formal analysis, <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/data-curation" class="topic-link">Data curation</a>. </span><strong>Hua Yang:</strong> Visualization, Validation. <strong>Jifei Liao:</strong> Writing â€“ review &amp; editing, Visualization, Formal analysis.</div></section></div><section id="coi1"><h2 id="d1e2133" class="u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Declaration of competing interest</h2><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2135">The authors declare that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.<