ems with Applications</div></div><div class="buttons"><button class="button-link button-link-primary button-link-icon-right" type="button" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby="citing-articles-article-0-title" aria-controls="citing-articles-article-0" aria-expanded="false"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Show abstract</span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"><div class="u-margin-ver-m"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="sp0001">Background clutter and extreme scale variations pose profound challenges for tiny person detection on construction sites. To address this, we introduce SRA-YOLO, an enhanced object detection model built upon the YOLO architecture, specifically designed to navigate the unstructured environmental noise inherent in ultra-long-range surveillance for construction safety. Our core innovation is the Riemannian Spatial Attention Convolution (RSAC), powered by the proposed Riemannian Metric Coordinate Attention (RMCA) mechanism. Unlike standard Euclidean attention methods, RMCA adaptively learns local metric tensors to measure feature proximity on a curved manifold, fundamentally mitigating feature collapse in microscopic targets. To complement this geometric enhancement, we integrate an Attention-guided Cross-domain Feature Pyramid (ACFP) that leverages spectral analysis to decouple high-frequency noise from semantic details within the frequency domain. Furthermore, we present the Distribution-Aware Dynamic Focusing Metric (DDFM) to resolve the vanishing gradient problem prevalent in tiny object regression. Experimental evaluations on our custom ULR-CPRD dataset demonstrate that this incrementally optimized architecture delivers robust, high-precision detection with a minimal parameter footprint (3.09M), effectively optimizing the trade-off between localization accuracy and computational efficiency. Beyond algorithmic improvements, we extend SRA-YOLO into a real-time risk area intrusion detection system. By integrating the ByteTrack algorithm with a homography-based spatiotemporal projection strategy, this system translates 2D pixel-level bounding boxes into reliable 3D physical risk assessments. Extensive testing on the VisDrone benchmark and the construction-specific C-MOT dataset confirms the modelâ€™s superior generalization and highly stable multi-object tracking capability, fully satisfying the stringent demands of industrial edge deployment.</div></div></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-1-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0952197626000837"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">An unmanned aerial vehicles small target detection algorithm based on dynamic pruning</span></span></a></h3><div>2026, Engineering Applications of Artificial Intelligence</div></div><div class="buttons"><button class="button-link button-link-primary button-link-icon-right" type="button" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby="citing-articles-article-1-title" aria-controls="citing-articles-article-1" aria-expanded="false"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Show abstract</span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"><div class="u-margin-ver-m"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="abspara0010">The integration of drone technology with visual detection techniques has opened up new opportunities in the field of deep learning-based object detection. However, these opportunities are often accompanied by significant challenges. Images captured from drone perspectives typically exhibit characteristics such as small target sizes, complex backgrounds, and frequent occlusions, which collectively hinder detection performance. Moreover, most existing approaches are based on incremental improvements to established algorithmic strategies. However, these improvements often overlook the inherent limitations of the original algorithms in detecting small objects, leading to only marginal gains in accuracy while increasing the complexity of network structures. To address these challenges, we propose a dynamic pruning-based small object detection algorithm for Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs), named Dynamic Pruning for UAV Detection-You Only Look Once (DPUD-YOLO). First, we introduce a feature fusion module called Adaptive Enhanced Spatial Pyramid Pooling (AESPPF), which enhances attention to small object features by integrating the Shuffle Attention mechanism. It further incorporates global average pooling and max pooling to enrich contextual information and extract multi-scale features. In addition, we modify the neck's output structure by removing low-resolution outputs that are unsuitable for small object detection and introducing high-resolution outputs. Based on this, we upgrade the coupled detection head to an efficient and lightweight decoupled detection head (EL-Decoupled Head), which separates regression and classification tasks to improve detection accuracy. In particular, we design a feature extraction module named Dynamic Pruning Bottleneck (DPB), which optimizes feature processing by pruning redundant information while retaining essential shallow features, thereby achieving efficient feature extraction and organization. Experimental results on the VisDrone2019 dataset demonstrate that our algorithm achieves a mean average precision (<a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="mailto:mAP@0.5" target="_blank"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">mAP@0.5</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a>) of 41.8&nbsp;%, outperforming the baseline, You Only Look Once version 8 (YOLOv8), You Only Look Once version 9 (YOLOv9), and You Only Look Once version 10 (YOLOv10) by 8.7&nbsp;%, 2.6&nbsp;%, 0.5&nbsp;%, and 2.2&nbsp;%, respectively. These results indicate the superior detection performance of our method. Furthermore, improvements in other evaluation metrics are consistently supported by evidence from both ablation and comparative experiments.</div></div></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-2-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0926580526000038"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Path planning for UAV-based construction safety inspection under spatiotemporal interference from tower cranes</span></span></a></h3><div>2026, Automation in Construction</div></div><div class="buttons"><button class="button-link button-link-primary button-link-icon-right" type="button" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby="citing-articles-article-2-title" aria-controls="citing-articles-article-2" aria-expanded="false"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Show abstract</span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"><div class="u-margin-ver-m"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="sp0105">Construction sites are dynamic, complex, high-risk environments, where Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) are vital for enhancing safety inspection efficiency. As large-scale dynamic obstacles, tower cranes can interfere with effective UAV inspection paths. This paper proposes a safety inspection path planning method under the spatiotemporal interference of multiple tower cranes. First, a 3D model of the construction site is reconstructed, and inspection viewpoints for UAV flights are generated by optimizing safety inspection strategies. Then, a hierarchical path planning framework is established: the lower-level planner strictly enforces real-time safety obstacle avoidance strategies, while the higher-level planner focuses on global planning to meet inspection requirements. Finally, both simulation and real project studies are conducted to verify the feasibility of the method. Results from the real project show that the effective coverage area is increased by 39.01&nbsp;% compared with traditional methods. This paper provides theoretical and practical support for UAV-assisted safety inspections in construction.</div></div></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-3-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0926580525005230"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Data-centric enhancement of site-specific automated construction equipment detection in wide-angle site images</span></span></a></h3><div>2025, Automation in Construction</div></div><div class="buttons"><button class="button-link button-link-primary button-link-icon-right" type="button" data-aa-button="sd:product:journal:article:location=citing-articles:type=view-details" aria-describedby="citing-articles-article-3-title" aria-controls="citing-articles-article-3" aria-expanded="false"><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Show abstract</span></span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 92 128" height="20" class="icon icon-navigate-down"><path d="M1 51l7-7 38 38 38-38 7 7-45 45z"></path></svg></button></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"><div class="u-margin-ver-m"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="sp0060">Construction equipment detection in wide-angle site images is limited by data scarcity, site-specific variability, and adaptability of existing models. This paper presents a site-specific automated framework from a data-centric perspective to enhance detection performance. Site-specific datasets are generated using zero-shot instance segmentation and depth estimation to create synthetic images that reflect actual site conditions. Object detection models are trained on these datasets, and a slicing-based inference pipeline is integrated to further improve detection performance. Four model configurations are compared: combining two equipment image types (bounding-box and segmented objects) and two synthetic methods (scale-agnostic and scale-aware). The framework improves detection performance by up to 13.72&nbsp;% over the control group. Requiring minimal human intervention, it offers a reproducible and scalable approach for developing site-specific object detection models, supporting downstream applications such as productivity analysis and safety monitoring.</div></div></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-4-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-025-31392-5" target="_blank"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Real-time construction safety monitoring using a drone based deep hybrid attention model</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></h3><div>2026, Scientific Reports</div></div><div class="buttons"></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"></div></div></li><li class="ListArticleItem u-margin-l-bottom"><div class="sub-heading u-margin-xs-bottom"><h3 class="u-font-serif" id="citing-articles-article-5-title"><a class="anchor anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.3390/buildings15234274" target="_blank"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">HFE-YOLO: Hybrid Feature Enhancement with Multi-Attention Mechanisms for Construction Site Object Detection</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></h3><div>2025, Buildings</div></div><div class="buttons"></div><div class="u-display-none" aria-hidden="true"><div class="abstract u-margin-xs-top u-margin-m-bottom u-font-serif" id="reference-abstract"></div></div></li></ul><a class="button-alternative button-alternative-secondary large-alternative button-alternative-icon-left" href="http://www.scopus.com/scopus/inward/citedby.url?partnerID=10&amp;rel=3.0.0&amp;eid=2-s2.0-85217280077&amp;md5=8c80567f188d163534c985f23bc888" target="_blank" id="citing-articles-v